# `vercel --prod` runs from inside this folder (see docs/DEPLOYMENT_WEB.md), and it uploads the
# working tree rather than HEAD. `.env.local` must not be part of that: Next loads it *over*
# `.env.production` in every environment including a production build, so a developer's
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5080` would be inlined into the deployed bundle. The
# build would still succeed — next.config.ts only rejects an empty value — and the console would
# go live fully styled, talking to a machine that isn't there.
.env.local
.env*.local

# Build output and local caches: Vercel builds these itself.
.next
node_modules
*.tsbuildinfo
